== J.1 Chapter 1 — Scarcity and Choice

+ *“Opportunity cost is the money paid.”* It works because money is the visible sacrifice. Correction: opportunity cost is the *next best alternative forgone*; the money paid is the money cost, a different number.
+ *“Add up every alternative.”* It works because it sounds thorough. Correction: only the *next best* counts. The others were never going to be chosen.
+ *“A point inside the PPC is efficient because it is attainable.”* Correction: attainable is not efficient. Every point *on* the curve is efficient; inside means idle resources.
+ *“A movement along the PPC is growth.”* Correction: growth is a *shift* of the whole curve. A movement along it is a reallocation.
+ *“Money is capital.”* Correction: money buys capital but produces nothing itself. Capital is produced means of production.
+ *“Land earns profit.”* Correction: the four rewards are fixed — land rent, labour wages, capital interest, entrepreneurship profit.

== J.2 Chapter 2 — Demand and Utility

+ *“A price rise reduces demand.”* Correction: it reduces *quantity demanded*. Demand is the whole curve, and only a determinant shifts it.
+ *“A change in income moves the consumer along the curve.”* Correction: income shifts the curve. Only the good's own price moves along it.
+ *“Giffen and Veblen are the same.”* Correction: Giffen works through the income effect on an inferior staple; Veblen works through display. Both break the law, for different reasons.
+ *“Total utility is maximised where marginal utility is maximised.”* Correction: TU peaks where MU is *zero*. MU peaks at the first unit.
+ *“A fall in the price of flour shifts the demand for bread.”* Correction: flour is an input, so it shifts the *supply* of bread.
+ *“Demand is a quantity.”* Correction: demand is a schedule or a curve; a quantity is one point on it.

== J.3 Chapter 3 — Supply and Equilibrium

+ *“A tax shifts supply right.”* Correction: a tax is a cost, so supply shifts *left*. A subsidy shifts it right.
+ *“A price ceiling above equilibrium causes a shortage.”* Correction: a ceiling above equilibrium is not binding at all. Only a ceiling *below* equilibrium bites.
+ *“A price floor below equilibrium causes a surplus.”* Correction: a floor below equilibrium is not binding; the market price is already above it.
+ *“Demand rises, so price falls.”* Correction: demand rising shifts the curve right, so price and quantity both *rise*. A demand *fall* lowers price.
+ *“Both curves moved, so both price and quantity are certain.”* Correction: one of the two is always indeterminate unless the question says which shift is larger.
+ *“Supply is the stock in the warehouse.”* Correction: supply is what is offered at each price; stock is inventory.



== J.4 Chapter 4 — Elasticity

+ *“Divide the other way.”* It works because both divisions look plausible. Correction: quantity always goes on top, price on the bottom.
+ *“Use absolute changes.”* Correction: elasticity compares *percentages*. 10 units out of 100 is not the same as 10 units out of 20.
+ *“PED is negative, so demand is inelastic.”* Correction: the paper quotes PED as a positive number. The *size* decides elastic or inelastic; the sign is ignored.
+ *“A price rise raises revenue when demand is elastic.”* Correction: elastic demand means quantity falls proportionately more, so revenue *falls*.
+ *“A negative income elasticity makes a good a complement.”* Correction: income elasticity names normal, necessity, luxury or inferior. Cross elasticity names substitutes and complements.
+ *“The tax falls on whoever the law names.”* Correction: the burden falls on the side that cannot escape — the *inelastic* side — whatever the statute says.

== J.5 Chapter 5 — Production and Cost

+ *“Marginal cost is average cost of one unit.”* Correction: marginal is the *change* in total cost, not a cost per unit.
+ *“MC cuts AC at its maximum.”* Correction: at its *minimum*. Below that point MC drags AC down; above it, MC pulls AC up.
+ *“Fixed cost belongs in marginal cost.”* Correction: fixed cost does not change with output, so it never enters MC.
+ *“Shut down when price is below average cost.”* Correction: shut down below minimum *average variable* cost. Between AVC and AC the firm loses money but loses less by producing.
+ *“Economies of scale are diminishing returns.”* Correction: economies of scale is a long-run statement about average cost; diminishing returns is a short-run law about marginal product.
+ *“Break-even and profit maximisation are the same point.”* Correction: break-even is TR = TC; maximum profit is MR = MC, and it lies beyond break-even.

== J.6 Chapter 6 — Market Structures

+ *“A monopolist sets price equal to marginal cost.”* Correction: produce where MR = MC, then read the price off the *demand* curve — which is above MC.
+ *“Read the price off the marginal revenue curve.”* Correction: MR gives the output. The price comes from the demand curve at that output.
+ *“A perfectly competitive firm earns supernormal profit in the long run.”* Correction: free entry competes it away. Long-run profit is normal only.
+ *“Perfect competition allows differentiated products.”* Correction: the product is homogeneous. Differentiation belongs to monopolistic competition.
+ *“A monopolist charges the highest price possible.”* Correction: the highest price sells nothing. The monopolist chooses the price that maximises profit, which is a compromise between price and quantity.
+ *“Normal profit means no profit at all.”* Correction: normal profit is the minimum return that keeps the entrepreneur in the industry, and it is included in cost. Zero *economic* profit is normal profit.

#v(5pt)

#shortcut-box("The five-second self-check before you shade an option")[
  + *Did the question change a price or a determinant?* Price means movement; determinant means shift.
  + *Is the answer a percentage or a naira amount?* Elasticity is a ratio; revenue and cost are money.
  + *Which side cannot escape?* The inelastic side bears the tax and the adjustment.
  + *Is it a total or a change?* Marginal means change in a total.
  + *Does the direction match the shift?* A rightward demand shift raises price and quantity; a leftward supply shift raises price and cuts quantity.
]

